In article <31bdc1$dp8@dawn.mmm.com>, us296698@mmm.com (SEAN CAREY) writes:
> Joe Maglitta (joem@mail.cw.com) wrote:
> 
>> Anybody know how a young person (18) can get started or educated in a 
>> career creating computer games ? A new newphew (through marriage) asked 
>> me about schools, programs, companies that consider neophytes, etc. This 
>> is not strictly a bug, but a problem. Any help appreciated. 
> 
> You may want to ask this question in rec.games.programmer.  There are
> (or seem to be) quite a few experienced game programmers who frequent that
> newsgroup.  From my understanding, though, the only school that actually
> teaches games development is in Japan.  The usual recommendation is to
> get a graphics library (there are several shareware libraries out there
> such as WordupGraphicsToolkit 3.0 or 4.0) and just start programming some
> games.  Of course, your nephew will need some knowledge of C programming
> to get started.  If he's planning to go to college (strongly encouraged :-) )
> then there are courses in graphics programming and algorithm development
> that will also help.  Learning assembly programming and how to program the
> vga card would also be useful, and there are several good books on these
> subjects.  Anyway, I'm not a professional game developer, but I've played
> around with it some.  The biggest things he'll need, though is patience and
> the desire to learn about game programming for himself.
> 
> Good luck!
--
Theres also an MSc course being offered in the UK, but like Mr Carey wrote, the
usual way to get started is to create some games or demos yourself and send
them to the software houses. I suppose the best things to learn is stuff
like animation and modelling algorithms, game theory, how to write fast, tight
code etc. Might be worth learning assembler, makes games faster, but makes
cross platform developement harder. Seems like game development is split up on
most big projects these days, with games designers, graphics, sound and the
engine being developed by seperate people or teams. Like Mr Carey, I'm not a
professional games programmer (least not since the 1k ZX81 space invaders game
I wrote :) I'll volunteer as a play-tester though.

